      ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{6x+2y}{x}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x) \\\\ &=x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v&=\\frac{6x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v&=6+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=6+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{6+v}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{1}{6+v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v&=\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\ln|v+6|&=\\ln|x|+C_1 \\\\ |v+6|&=e^{C_1}|x| \\\\ v+6&=Cx \\\\ v&=Cx-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}&=Cx-6 \\\\ y&=Cx^2-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe signed constant $C$ absorbs $e^{C_1}$. Division by $v+6$ excluded $v=-6$; restoring it permits $C=0$, giving $y=-6x$.\n\nThe solution is used on intervals where $x\\ne0$.",
