      ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{2x^3+y^3}{xy^2}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x) \\\\ &=x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v&=\\frac{2x^3+(vx)^3}{x(vx)^2} \\\\ x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v&=\\frac{2+v^3}{v^2}=\\frac{2}{v^2}+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2}{v^2} \\\\ v^2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v^2\\,\\mathrm{d}v&=2\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{v^3}{3}&=2\\ln|x|+C_1 \\\\ v^3&=6\\ln|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)^3&=6\\ln|x|+C \\\\ y^3&=x^3(6\\ln|x|+C) \\\\ y&=x\\sqrt[3]{6\\ln|x|+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $C=3C_1$ and the cube root is real. Restrict the solution to intervals where $y\\ne0$.\n\nThe solution is used on intervals where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.",
